--TEST--
Inline HTML outside `<?nvs` … `?>` is written verbatim, and the one newline after `?>` is swallowed — spec 00-overview.md § 1
--FILE--
before any tag
<?nvs
// spec 00-overview.md § 1: a run of literal text outside code mode is written
// to the output exactly as its source bytes read — never escaped, never
// interpreted, unlike an `echo` operand. The line above is this file's first
// statement, so a template needs no opening tag at all.
echo "in code\n";
?>
verbatim <b>&amp;</b> — no escaping, no conversion
<?nvs
// The lexer swallows one newline immediately after `?>`, so the run above
// ends in exactly one `\n`: a template line ending in `?>` emits no blank
// line of its own.
int $i = 0;
for (; $i < 2; ) {
    // `?>`/`<?nvs` reopen and reclose code mode anywhere a statement is
    // expected, not only at file scope, so a run inside a loop body is
    // written once per iteration.
    ?>row <?nvs echo $i as string; ?>!
<?nvs
    $i = $i + 1;
}
// A `?>` followed by nothing but the newline it swallows is an *empty* run,
// and it contributes no output at all.
?>
<?nvs
echo "end\n";
?>
after the last tag
--EXPECT--
before any tag
in code
verbatim <b>&amp;</b> — no escaping, no conversion
row 0!
row 1!
end
after the last tag
